WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.670
I'm Akshay Kothari. I'm the co-founder and COO of Notion.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.047
Notion is a productivity software company.

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:10.176
And what we're trying to do is essentially
provide you one simple medium where you can essentially

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:14.806
do all your work, everything from note taking
to having a knowledge base for a company,

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:17.183
managing your projects all in a single medium.

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:22.188
We've done this by giving people building
blocks for what these apps are built from.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:26.693
Before this, I was at LinkedIn for many years
when they acquired my previous startup Pulse.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.027
I'm excited to chat with you today.

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:40.123
I was really good at math and physics,
and I studied electrical engineering when I came to the US.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.625
And I actually did a lot of engineering internships.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.086
It turns out that I didn't enjoy it that much.

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:49.257
I found the engineering work
to be a bit too sort of granular.

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:51.384
I haven't really coded in a long time.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:55.221
I'm much more interested in product
and business and the intersection of that.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:59.726
I wanted to sort of take a step back
and think about maybe more larger, higher level sort

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:04.355
of product business things that I could be working
on in between my bachelor's degree,

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:07.108
in my master's degree, I spent a year in venture capital.

00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:11.863
That's actually for me was a very important year
because I got to meet a lot of entrepreneurs.

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:16.326
After hearing the pitches for about a year,
I realized that I wanted to be on the other side

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:20.830
and not hearing the pitches as an investor,
but actually wanting to start something of my own.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.081
That took me to Stanford.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:25.668
So I went to Stanford for two years every quarter,
tried to start a company.

00:01:25.668 --> 00:01:31.424
Many of them failed, but in my last quarter I started Pulse,
which was a essentially like a news

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.511
application built for the iPad back in 2010.

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:38.181
We built this as a class project at Stanford.
We built this pretty fast.

00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:40.391
We had to, in the whole class was 11 weeks, 

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:44.604
so we built this sort of hacked up version
of Pulse in about six weeks,

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:47.982
and we sort of put it out in the App Store; not sure how it would do.

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:50.944
In a couple of weeks.
It sort of went up to the top of the App Store.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:55.406
A few weeks later,
Steve Jobs talked about it and the WWDC in 2010.

00:01:55.406 --> 00:01:59.536
And again, let me just show you some of the the latest
apps that have been out, Pulse,

00:01:59.536 --> 00:02:02.455
which is a wonderful RSS reader, if you haven't seen it.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.959
And overnight it went from like a class
project to being a company.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:09.671
I think the big learnings one is that when you're
building something, I think you sort of my

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:13.174
natural inclination was to like not release publicly.

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:14.509
I wanted to make things better.

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:18.680
I think if I had done that, I think I probably would
have never released, I think the professor in the class.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.101
I remember Michael Deering pushed me to like release
even the beta version of the product we had.

00:02:23.101 --> 00:02:25.979
And by putting it out there,
we got so much feedback, we improved it,

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.856
we got in the market, we became a company.

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:31.442
So I think sort of bias towards action
of actually shipping something quickly.

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:32.902
It was worked out really well for us.

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:36.781
I think the second thing that I learned was
that many times like we are much more

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:41.452
interested in becoming an entrepreneur or starting a company
rather than actually solving a problem.

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:46.457
And if I think about Pulse versus the previous things
I had done, Pulse was actually solving a real problem

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:51.129
that existed, which was that it was
impossible to read news on your phone back in 2010.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:55.175
I think if you build something that people want
that people are struggling with,

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:57.135
I think they will come and actually use the product.

00:02:57.135 --> 00:03:00.930
And then probably the last thing I would say
that I think product is only half of the puzzle.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:05.101
I think you have to think about distribution
and how you take the product to market.

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:09.022
You know, that's something that we use
that I think a lot about Notion like we could be building

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:13.318
the best possible product in this office,
but if we don't figure out a way to distribute our product,

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:15.945
like people will never know what we've built.

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:18.781
In college.

00:03:18.781 --> 00:03:24.579
I studied cognitive science, how a human brain works,
and a little bit computing a little bit philosophy.

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:27.540
But I really got into fine art as well, photography.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.753
At the end of my school years,
most my friends are artists.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:36.299
I'm the only person who know how to code in my friends circle,
so I start building web pages for everyone.

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:40.762
After making those three, four or four
or five websites, those people are creative.

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:43.598
They can build their own websites
if they know how to do that.

00:03:43.598 --> 00:03:47.018
Computing as a medium, they don't know
how to speak that media.

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:51.064
With Spotify as a tool, everybody can play music.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:56.069
At that stage, I was very much thinking about how can
I help other people to create their websites.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:01.950
Ivan was based in Canada and he decided
to come to the Bay Area in San Francisco In 2011.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:07.747
He put out a simple post in Hacker News which basically said like,
Hey, I'm a designer, I'm looking for a job.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:11.834
I want to work in San Francisco.
And I essentially wanted to hire him as a designer.

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:16.673
And we actually gave him an offer to join Pulse back in 2011,
but he ended up joining another company.

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:22.720
But we kept in touch, and after I had sold Pulse
to LinkedIn in 2013, he was about to start Notion.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.308
Notion ended up being my first investment.
I had no intentions to joining full time.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:28.810
I think that happened five years later.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:33.189
I think even before he started Notion,
I think he was thinking about this problem for many years.

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:39.445
He deeply believes that everyone can be
a creator and that computing devices are

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.531
built for creation, not just consumption.

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:46.536
To me, that felt like a really compelling mission
because if you can actually make it happen,

00:04:46.536 --> 00:04:49.288
then you can unleash a lot of creativity in the world.

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:52.625
In many ways, when you're investing in early stage,
I think you're sort of like

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:57.046
betting on the person much more than the idea,
because the idea can actually change.

00:04:57.046 --> 00:05:02.385
For that specific thing, It was very easy to bet on Ivan because you could
see the passion and sort of deep thinking he

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.721
had done before, even before he started notion.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:10.018
At your environment.
Those are the documents you have in motion.

00:05:10.018 --> 00:05:13.938
If we try to reorder stuff,
you just drag it where you think it should be.

00:05:13.938 --> 00:05:16.858
The very first version of Notion is a web page builder.

00:05:16.858 --> 00:05:21.696
That's the prototype.
And from there evolve into a web app builder.

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:24.282
That's pretty much the first few years of Notion.

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:28.578
It took me several years to realize
that people don't want to build apps.

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:32.081
Very few people want to do that.
It's kind of collaborative building apps.

00:05:32.081 --> 00:05:35.877
It's kind of like a document editing.
It's kind of like Xcode for the web.

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:36.878
It's very confusing.

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:41.299
On top of that, we're building on top of some
technical foundation that wasn't stable,

00:05:41.299 --> 00:05:46.429
so it was very hard to tell whether it's our own bug
or it's the bug come from the platform we're using.

00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:49.640
So fundamentally, we have to rewrite the whole software.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.393
We're going to run out of time
if we don't reset the company.

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:54.771
I think the most important one is about yourself.

00:05:54.771 --> 00:06:00.735
It's about are you building something for yourself
or are you building something that the world wants?

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:05.948
The first person notion very much what you
think the world wants, what you want.

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:10.703
So notions first approach was essentially to create
this platform where you can build software.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:14.957
So it gave you all the building blocks and it basically said,
okay, now you can take these building blocks

00:06:14.957 --> 00:06:17.168
and build whatever software you can dream about.

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:20.213
The challenge is that people don't
wake up wanting to build software.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.340
People just wake up to do their job.

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:26.344
At that time team is about four or five people.

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:30.681
Me and Sam and my co-founders would probably most
likely going to run out of money, unfortunately,

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:37.063
layoff everybody and go to somewhere small and interesting
and quiet and we can just focus on coding.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:41.567
Look out of Japan, because neither of us has been
to Japan and we always want to go to.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:48.324
And the reason for Kyoto is if you look at on Airbnb,
Tokyo and Osaka or other major cities,

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:51.160
the rooms are fairly small, the apartments are fairly small.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.997
But for Kyoto, probably because during
World War Two it wasn't got bombed

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:57.458
and a lot of older houses are still there.

00:06:57.458 --> 00:07:01.671
They're quite a bit bigger.
And the pace of life in the city is slower as well.

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:06.008
So for folks, I'm working on things
and it's a much cheaper city than San Francisco.

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:11.514
So rebuild the Notion that everybody is
working on today is rebuilt from that stage.

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:18.271
So in 2018 summer, Ivan and I got
together for breakfast just to catch up.

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:23.151
I was at LinkedIn then I was itching to get back
into the garage and build again and I thought I would

00:07:23.151 --> 00:07:25.486
probably leave LinkedIn and start my own company.

00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:29.532
But as you were having breakfast heat,
I haven't talked a lot about the 2.0 release

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:30.825
which introduced the database.

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:36.747
The tipping point for us was when we moved away
from asking you to build software to providing you

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:38.875
simple templates that allow you to do your job.

00:07:38.875 --> 00:07:42.712
We came in and said, Hey, here's a knowledge base
so you can collaborate with all your friends.

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:45.840
Now, underneath that product,
all the capabilities still exist.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.762
Like you can still modify it, you can still build
your own software, but my marketing to you is less

00:07:51.762 --> 00:07:57.768
about that and more about specific things that you need
to do your job and how notion fills that gap.

00:07:57.768 --> 00:08:03.191
And so as we were talking, Ivan was at that time looking
for a COO who could really help scale the business.

00:08:03.191 --> 00:08:08.279
The company was about eight people then,
and he offered me that role, which was a really interesting role.

00:08:08.279 --> 00:08:12.033
I think everything that I had done up until that point
was more on the product side,

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:17.538
and this role was basically a role that was trying
to do everything inside the company except product.

00:08:17.538 --> 00:08:21.918
I think his thinking was that if he could get
a product counterpart to really help build our

00:08:21.918 --> 00:08:27.256
go to market and the foundational teams here,
I think it would allow him to stay focused on product.

00:08:27.256 --> 00:08:31.594
And for me this felt like a really interesting
opportunity where I joined well before.

00:08:31.594 --> 00:08:35.223
I think most people knew about Notion
and I got to take something that had just hit

00:08:35.223 --> 00:08:37.141
product market fit and take it to the masses.

00:08:37.141 --> 00:08:41.896
After a lot of thinking, I decided to join
in the fall of 2018 and it's been amazing to watch.

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:45.316
I think we were eight people then
and we are close to 500 people now.

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:49.570
Just in the four years business has
grown over 100 fold in that time frame.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.573
As the first Notion consultant in Korea

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:58.287
I believe the key to Notion’s global success is Notion’s user community worldwide. 

00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:02.917
I discovered Notion on ProductHunt
during the release of Notion 2.0.

00:09:02.917 --> 00:09:07.463
After using the product I fell in love with Notion's database function.

00:09:07.463 --> 00:09:12.552
I then took to social media posts to share my knowledge, knowhow,

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:18.474
and templates and guides to contribute to growth of 
the Notion community in South Korea.

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:22.186
Today the community has 43K members. 

00:09:22.186 --> 00:09:27.316
It’s amazing to see similar Notion communities popping up all over the world.

00:09:27.316 --> 00:09:33.114
It's kind of like art combined with technology,
it's very intuition driven.

00:09:33.114 --> 00:09:38.494
So a lot of things that just feels right
and other things we try to keep things very,

00:09:38.494 --> 00:09:41.706
we call it keep a extremely simple, the simplest.

00:09:41.706 --> 00:09:46.794
We use the word dumb, like so dumb that you need
to pay too much attention to start picking

00:09:46.794 --> 00:09:48.921
up and start using it because it feels really natural.

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:53.634
It should just feels like your extension of your body.
You know how to use your hands.

00:09:53.634 --> 00:10:00.725
You know how to use very simple tools like a pen
or a cup because they're evolved so long with you.

00:10:00.725 --> 00:10:05.813
A fundamental is about solving people's problems.
If we do so, then people should love us.

00:10:05.813 --> 00:10:10.318
They will love us. And and there's a lot more room to grow.

00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:14.447
You can do so many different things with Notion,
but it's built by just over 100 engineers.

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:21.162
And I think the reason that is possible is
because architecturally we have built the sort of the Notion product

00:10:21.162 --> 00:10:26.876
in a way that is very modular and we sort of want
to expose these building blocks to other people.

00:10:26.876 --> 00:10:33.132
For example, we are not building a CRM
or we are not building a specific project management software,

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:36.135
but we're giving you all
the building blocks so that you can build it.

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:41.140
And so similar to Lego, I think the engineers focus
on building the building blocks and then we have

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:47.521
the marketing team and the community that takes the building blocks
and creates templates that market specific use cases,

00:10:47.521 --> 00:10:49.732
and that makes it a very efficient engine.

00:10:49.732 --> 00:10:52.443
Engineers don't have to think
about building a very specific use case.

00:10:52.443 --> 00:10:57.156
They focus on just building capability
and then marketing and other stakeholders build

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:59.367
a packaging that we can go out and sell.

00:10:59.367 --> 00:11:02.161
I think that's probably one of the reasons
why a Notion has been able to stay

00:11:02.161 --> 00:11:05.247
so lean as we've grown to scale so far.

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:12.088
Today we're introducing notion A.I.,
which brings the power of artificial intelligence

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:14.131
directly into your Notion workspace.

00:11:15.633 --> 00:11:18.427
Let's say we're writing a blog post to introduce Notion AI.

00:11:18.427 --> 00:11:23.307
It's a simple as asking AI assist for help
and clicking generate post.

00:11:24.266 --> 00:11:29.063
Next, just sit back and watch
as artificial intelligence completes your first draft.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:38.114
Notion AI allows people to essentially just tell what you're trying
to do and it sort of automatically generates a lot

00:11:38.114 --> 00:11:40.408
of content or does a lot of things that you wanted to.

00:11:40.408 --> 00:11:43.452
If you have a long doc,
you can ask it to summarize things.

00:11:43.452 --> 00:11:46.038
If you are starting from scratch,
it's like a blank page.

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:50.960
You want some help, you can ask it to write a few things
so that you can get started with it.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.714
I think AI itself is changing every week.
I'm excited to see how it evolves.

00:11:54.714 --> 00:11:58.884
It's been quite a fun journey,
but at the same time it still feels like pretty early.

00:11:58.884 --> 00:12:03.472
Like I think there's still a lot of work to be done
and we still feel like our best days are ahead of us.

00:12:03.472 --> 00:12:06.559
So I'm excited to see
what the next couple of years hold for us.

00:12:09.228 --> 00:12:13.816
We have four values at Notion.
We are mission driven owners of our mission.

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:18.237
So essentially our mission is to sort
of enable ubiquitous software tool making,

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:20.239
and we very much are owners of that.

00:12:20.239 --> 00:12:24.827
The second value is that we are pacesetters,
obviously a startup, so we need to move fast.

00:12:24.827 --> 00:12:27.204
I think that's one way we can win in the market.

00:12:27.204 --> 00:12:32.501
The third value is we are truth seekers,
which is that we spend a lot of time rigorously thinking about

00:12:32.501 --> 00:12:35.129
a specific decision before we actually move forward.

00:12:35.129 --> 00:12:38.716
And the fourth one is we are kind and direct.

00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:42.678
We want to be kind to our coworkers,
but at the same time we want to be able to be

00:12:42.678 --> 00:12:47.516
direct and be honest with each other so that we're
pushing each other to do our best work.

00:12:47.516 --> 00:12:50.769
I think these four values are
very much authentically us.

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:53.606
It's not aspirationally us,
but it's like who we are.

00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:57.818
And we like that the second and third value, which is pace setting
and truth seeking fight with each other,

00:12:57.818 --> 00:13:00.696
because in many cases some projects you want
to move fast and in some cases you want

00:13:00.696 --> 00:13:03.324
to be able to take time and really rigorously think.

00:13:03.324 --> 00:13:07.661
So we like that they fight with each other so that we find like,
okay, which projects can we move fast with?

00:13:07.661 --> 00:13:09.872
Which projects to we spend more time thinking about?

00:13:09.872 --> 00:13:14.168
The fourth value of being kind and direct is obviously
nice because we're not just trying to be nice with each other,

00:13:14.168 --> 00:13:19.006
we're trying to really achieve our mission
and that requires us to be direct with each other.

00:13:19.006 --> 00:13:21.884
This is these values have really
helped us hire the right people.

00:13:21.884 --> 00:13:25.971
These values have really helped us make
being a good decision sort of rubric for us.

00:13:25.971 --> 00:13:30.768
Like when we're making important decisions,
we think about like, does this represent the values we have?

00:13:30.768 --> 00:13:31.644
And it's memorable.

00:13:31.644 --> 00:13:36.482
I can say these four values in my sleep now,
which I think is nice because I don't have

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:37.775
to think about what the values are.

00:13:37.775 --> 00:13:42.988
There was a long term sort of mission of the company is
to create ubiquitous software tool making,

00:13:42.988 --> 00:13:46.367
which is the simple idea
that there's a billion knowledge workers out there.

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:52.957
And our hope is that all of them feel the power
to modify or create their own software.

00:13:52.957 --> 00:13:57.753
In doing that, we hope that Notion becomes
the third generation of productivity software.

00:13:57.753 --> 00:14:01.882
After Microsoft Office and Google Suite,
we're much more powerful in that,

00:14:01.882 --> 00:14:06.637
people will be able to not just take notes
and manage projects, but we'll be able to create things

00:14:06.637 --> 00:14:09.139
that sort of work the way their brain works.

00:14:09.139 --> 00:14:10.349
So very excited about that.

00:14:10.349 --> 00:14:13.602
It'll probably take us more than a decade to get there,
but we're excited to work on it.

